{
  "answer": "Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 support continuing, ultimate Product Provider responsibility despite outsourcing. They do not supply a complete test for fulfillment. A justified conclusion would require an applicable standard and evidence connecting retained authority, actual supervisory conduct, and operational soundness and ownership records to that standard throughout the relevant period. Allocation documents, a supervision event, or a completed reconciliation may support parts of that assessment; none alone establishes fulfillment.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "Text: outsourcing does not remove the Product Provider’s responsibility. Deduction: delegating performance must be distinguished from transferring ultimate accountability. Whether the requirement applies to a particular entity remains a separate question.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The entity is the relevant Product Provider.",
        "The retained edition applies to the product, activity and assessment period, accounting for relevant conditions and amendments."
      ],
      "proposition": "Ultimate responsibility remains with the Product Provider notwithstanding outsourcing.",
      "status": "Supported by retained text; individual applicability unestablished",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Proposed evidential judgment: contracts, governance arrangements and actual decision-making could establish who retains accountability and effective authority, including access to information and ability to require correction. These are candidate evidence, not an express statutory checklist or sufficient discharge test supplied by these passages.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "An applicable standard specifying what retained responsibility requires in this arrangement.",
        "Actual contractual allocations, governance arrangements, information rights and intervention powers.",
        "Evidence that stated powers remain effective in practice."
      ],
      "proposition": "Allocation of authority can support retained responsibility but does not by itself establish satisfactory performance.",
      "status": "Reasoned evidential proposal; sufficiency unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The scheme, or in the case of a unit trust, the trustee or the person so appointed by the trustee must maintain a register of holders.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Deduction: an event log proves at most the recorded activity, subject to reliability; adequacy requires assessing its substance against a relevant standard. UT 6.11C expressly addresses supervision but concerns outsourced valuation by a management company. Its standard cannot automatically become the paragraph 10 test for every Product Provider.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "A justified standard for the scope, frequency and quality of supervision.",
        "Evidence of competent review, identified risks, escalation, corrective action and follow-through over the relevant period.",
        "If relying on UT 6.11C, its applicability to the actor and valuation activity, or a justified analogical transfer."
      ],
      "proposition": "Actual supervisory conduct is distinct from allocated authority, and a recorded supervision event alone does not establish adequate supervision.",
      "status": "Supported distinction; adequacy unestablished",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "For factors of consideration, please also refer to Part A of the Appendix to the Tokenised Securities Circular, where applicable, when formulating the tokenisation arrangement.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Where a third party is engaged in the valuation of a scheme, the management company shall exercise reasonable care, skill and diligence in the selection, appointment and ongoing monitoring of such third party",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The valuation activities of such third party should be subject to ongoing supervision and periodic review by the management company.",
          "unit_id": "ut-current.parent"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: operational soundness and ownership record keeping are substantive subjects of responsibility; paragraph 11 separately requires proper ownership-interest records and operational compatibility. Deduction: evidence of outcomes matters independently of authority and recorded activity. A completed reconciliation does not establish that its inputs reflect legally valid ownership, that discrepancies were resolved, or that coverage was adequate.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The applicable meaning and threshold of operational soundness and proper ownership records.",
        "The legal relationship between tokens, holders and interests in the product, including settlement finality.",
        "Reliable evidence of record accuracy, completeness, reconciliation scope and frequency, exception resolution, and compatibility with service providers.",
        "Operational evidence covering the relevant risks and period rather than a single successful event."
      ],
      "proposition": "Operational and record-keeping outcomes require substantive assessment; successful activity completion is not a sufficient compliance criterion.",
      "status": "Source-grounded deduction; fulfillment unestablished",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        },
        {
          "quote": "whether the ownership interest of the underlying assets of the Tokenised Securities are legally valid under the applicable governing laws, whether there are regular reconciliations between the records for the extrinsic rights and the records for the distributed ledger tokens, or whether any encumbrance affects the extrinsic rights.",
          "unit_id": "tokenised-securities.appendix"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "p10",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Allocation reading: 'remain' and 'ultimately responsible' primarily preserve accountability despite delegation. This supports non-transfer of responsibility but supplies no complete performance test.",
    "Conduct reading: responsibility for management entails effective oversight in practice. The passages do not specify an exhaustive supervisory standard or cadence.",
    "Outcome reading: responsibility for operational soundness and ownership record keeping requires substantively adequate operations and records. The text alone does not establish strict liability for every defect.",
    "Combined reading: authority, conduct and outcomes jointly inform fulfillment. This is a plausible assessment approach, not an expressly stated necessary-and-sufficient test."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "Which reading, or combination, supplies the applicable discharge standard and what legal authority supports that choice.",
    "Whether actual arrangements and performance satisfy that standard; no assessed Product Provider facts are supplied.",
    "How particular defects, remediation and time periods affect fulfillment while ultimate responsibility continues.",
    "Whether referenced UT provisions and Appendix factors apply directly or only inform an analogy; their distinct actors, activities and modalities must be preserved.",
    "Candidate rules, Boolean readings and argument-graph acceptance cannot establish legal sufficiency or replace missing legal and factual premises."
  ]
}
